  "notes": "The research calls this \"the lane's flagship group\" and gives the spine as a \"**69 half-time spine** on the Darkchild records (reads 138)\", so `halfTime` is true, the mode is 69 and the band runs 64–80 to hold the sourced 64 and 80 readings; the separate straight 96–119 band is a different backbeat class and is not folded in. Three drum deltas are the entry's template description: \"syncopated 808 programming with the kick on 16th offsets\" (syncopation 0.74, secondary anchor `2a`, three to five hits a bar), \"snare on 3\", and \"hats in gapped 16th bursts\" — a non-continuous 16th lane with a high roll frequency and a velocity ramp, which the research attributes to UK 2-step garage heard in a London club, hence a tight 0.54 swing rather than the genre's 0.62 and `uk-garage` in the related list. Harmony is \"two-chord minor-9th vamps with synthesized strings and 1970s wah-wah guitar licks as the colour; harpsichord/plucked-synth leads\", so ninth is the largest extension share and the families are two- and three-chord. \"Four-voice stacks used rhythmically — chopped, stabbed harmony rather than sustained pads\" is the staccato articulation and the riff-leaning contour. The low end is \"808 sub articulated in 16ths… entering and vanishing rather than sustaining\", with the harpsichord-synth carrying the low-mid on one record — articulated and non-sustaining, so it is the `bassline` block and there is no `drums.bass808` block, which would have silenced the part. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every velocity and density number, the swing figure and the melody register.",
